Effective Oceanic Angular Momentum Functions (OAM)

File name                    : ESMGFZ_OAM_v1.0_03h_1982.asc
File contents                : effective angular momentum functions from oceanic mass and motion terms
File units                   : dimensionless earth rotation excitation functions
Issue date                   : 2017-08-16
Version number               : v1.0
 
References                   : Dobslaw, H., R. Dill, A. Groetzsch, A. Brzezinski, and M. Thomas (2010), 
                               Seasonal polar motion excitation from numerical models of atmosphere, ocean, 
                               and continental hydrosphere, J. Geophys. Res., doi:10.1029/2009JB007127
 
Contact                      : Henryk Dobslaw, dobslaw@gfz-potsdam.de
Affiliation                  : Deutsches GeoForschungsZentrum GFZ
                               Dep. 1: Geodesy and Remote Sensing
                               Sec. 1.3: Earth System Modelling
                               Telegrafenberg
                               14473 Potsdam, Germany
 
Temporal domain              : 01.01.1982, 00:00 UTC until 31.12.1982, 21:00 UTC
Temporal resolution          : 3h 
Number of data records       : 2920
Subtracted mean values       : mean from 2003-01-01 to 2014-12-31
                               7.152098599129423e-17   1.847667664741510e-17   3.794620422326981e-20  -3.220254498203050e-08   8.226002041060833e-08   3.462197020095727e-09
 
Numerical model              : MPIOM (Max-Planck-Institute Ocean Model)
Horizontal domain            : global
Horizontal resolution        : 1.0 x 1.0 degree
 
Model forcing                : 3h ECMWF operational data
Correction                   : Oceanic pressure tides are subtracted, global mass (Greatbatch) correction applied
 
Data assimilated             : None

Notes : These AAMs are part of a set of consistently processed Earth rotation excitation 
        function (AAM, OAM, HAM). The ocean model MPIOM and the hydrological model LSDM were
        forced consistently with operational analysis data from the ECMWF.

